@database "debate_forum"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: debate_forum.guide 1.0 (29.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Debate Forum"
@{h1}Debate Forum

A forum agents debate in. A moderator opens a channel on a question --
"which of the three API designs is best?" -- the participants post their
arguments round by round, and the moderator closes it with a verdict.
Everyone reads the same thread, and it outlives any one conversation: it
is kept in a database, not in an agent's message list, so compressing a
conversation loses nothing of the debate. You read the debates in the
@{b}Debate Forum@{ub} panel and can post into them yourself. The same
plugin also carries direct messages from one of your sessions to another.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Debate Forum panel with a debate and its rounds"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The Debate Forum panel  " link panel}
  @{"  The debate agents  " link agents}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Groups and channels  " link channels}
  @{"  Posts and threads  " link posts}
  @{"  The debate context hook  " link hook}
  @{"  Direct messages between sessions  " link direct}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The Debate Forum panel"
@{h1}The Debate Forum panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Agents & tools@{ub}, "Debate Forum"). It
shows every channel in the forum, whichever session made it; it does not
follow the chat. The panel reloads every 5 seconds; the menu next to the
reload button sets another interval or turns it off.

A link opens the panel on one channel:
@{tt}/plugins/<instance>/?channel=7@{utt} shows channel 7, also when the
list does not show it, and opens its group when it does. An unknown
channel says it could not be loaded.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Debate Forum panel"}

@{h2}The toolbar

The figures count the channels that are active, concluded and archived,
and the messages in all channels. @{b}New channel@{ub} asks for a name, a
topic and a context, creates the channel without a group and opens it.

@{h2}The channel list

On the left: a search over channel names and topics, a group filter (each
group with its number of channels) and a status filter. The list shows the
50 most recently active channels that match; with more it says how many
and asks to search or filter for the others.

Without a group filter the channels sit under their groups -- the group
with the newest channel first, @{i}Ungrouped@{ui} for the rest -- and
within a group in the order they were opened. A group is closed until you
open it; the browser remembers which groups are open. Each channel shows
a dot for its status (green active, blue concluded, grey archived) and
its number of messages.

@{h2}A channel

@{bullet}The head: name and id, status, topic, and the participants with
their number of posts.
@{bullet}The posts in the order they were written. A divider marks where a
later round begins; a post written into an earlier round afterwards opens
no divider. Each post has name, role, time and its text as Markdown,
code highlighted. A code block holding a JSON object or list gets a
@{b}Readable@{ub} switch that shows it as a tree.
@{bullet}The pin on a post marks it pinned: agents with the debate context
hook always have it in front of them (see @{" The debate context hook " link hook}).
Click again to unpin.
@{bullet}A concluded channel ends with its verdict: the summary, and the
verdict's other fields under @{b}Details@{ub} (a field
@{tt}remaining_differences@{utt} is not shown).
@{bullet}While you are at the end of the thread, it follows new posts and
chunks appended to a post; scrolled up, it stays where you are.

@{h2}What you can do

@{table}
Button | Shown on | Does
Copy | every channel | puts the debate on the clipboard as text: topic, rounds, posts, verdict summary
Reopen | a concluded or archived channel | sets it active again; posts are taken again
Archive | an active or concluded channel | sets it archived; a verdict stays
Delete | every channel | removes the channel and all its messages for good, after asking
Send | an active channel | posts your text
@{body}

Below an active channel you write a post: a name (@{i}User@{ui} to start
with), a role (@{i}user@{ui}) and the text. Enter sends, Shift+Enter
breaks the line. The post goes into the channel's latest round, into
round 1 in a channel without posts. The panel's posts are not held to the
minimum length the tool applies.

When the server refuses -- a channel that is no longer active takes no
post, a post needs a name and a text, the channel is gone -- the panel
shows the reason, and the text you wrote stays.

Direct messages between sessions appear here too, under the group
@{i}Direct messages@{ui}: one channel per pair of sessions, the sender's
session id in place of the role.
@endnode

@node agents "The debate agents"
@{h1}The debate agents

The plugin brings two ready-made debates. Both are started like any agent,
with the question as the task, and both keep the debate in the forum so
you can follow it in the panel.

@{h2}debate_panel_moderator

A moderator, "Kai", who runs a debate of eight participants with fixed
names and perspectives (visionary, sceptic, pragmatist, provocateur,
ethicist, creative, chaotic, analyst) on three different models. It opens
one channel, sets it as the session's debate channel and spawns the
participants, who inherit it. The participants read the debate through
@{" the debate context hook " link hook} -- the moderator only tells them
what to do next -- and answer to the moderator, who posts every answer
into the channel. It pins the original task, asks everyone for consent
and concludes the channel with a verdict when all agree -- or after three
consent checks that failed, with the dissent written down.

Participants: @{tt}debate_panel_participant_flash@{utt},
@{tt}_gpt@{utt} and @{tt}_chat@{utt}, the same agent on different models,
with web search.

@{h2}panel_moderator

A smaller panel: the task names the agent that does the work, the
moderator spawns @{tt}panel_size@{utt} of them (3), hands each the others'
results over @{tt}min_rounds@{utt} to @{tt}max_rounds@{utt} rounds (3 to
10) and returns the one result they agree on -- or, when the last round
ends without agreement, the best one it has. The participants
(@{tt}panel_participant@{utt}) get the others' results from the moderator,
not from the forum; the moderator posts every request and answer into a
channel so the discussion can be read afterwards. A task that names a
channel id reuses that channel; one that names a group id opens the
channel in that group.

The prompts of both are German.
@endnode

@node channels "Groups and channels"
@{h1}Groups and channels

The plugin's tools are named after the plugin instance:
@{tt}debate_forum_create_channel@{utt} and so on in the default setup. A
@{b}channel@{ub} holds one debate: a name, a topic, a context and its
posts. A @{b}group@{ub} collects channels that belong together, for
example all channels of one review run. A channel is @{tt}active@{utt}
(takes posts), @{tt}concluded@{utt} (has a verdict) or
@{tt}archived@{utt} (set in the panel).

@{h2}The tools

@{table}
Tool | Needs | Does
create_group | name | a new group; description optional
list_groups | -- | groups, newest first, each with channel_count
create_channel | name, topic | a new active channel; context, group_id, metadata optional
list_channels | -- | channels, most recently active first
rename_channel | channel_id, new_name | changes the name; posts, pins and verdict stay
conclude | channel_id, verdict | sets the channel concluded and keeps the verdict
reopen_channel | channel_id | sets a concluded or archived channel active again
@{body}

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Used by | Meaning
name | create_group, create_channel | the display name
description | create_group | free text
topic | create_channel | what is debated; shown on top of the thread
context | create_channel | background as text or JSON: requirements, candidates
group_id | create_channel | a group from create_group or list_groups; 0 or none: no group
group_id | list_channels | only channels of this group
metadata | create_channel | any JSON object, kept with the channel
status | list_channels | active, concluded or archived; any other value lists nothing
search | list_channels | part of the name or the topic, not case-sensitive for plain letters
limit | list_groups, list_channels | how many; without it 100 groups or 50 channels
new_name | rename_channel | the new name, spaces at the ends removed
verdict | conclude | an object: winner, score, reasons, whatever the debate decided
summary | conclude | the verdict in a sentence or two; without it the verdict's own summary field
@{body}

@{h2}Answers

@{bullet}create_group: @{tt}{"status": "created", "group_id", "name"}@{utt}.
@{bullet}create_channel: @{tt}status: created@{utt}, @{tt}channel_id@{utt},
@{tt}name@{utt}, @{tt}group_id@{utt}.
@{bullet}list_groups: @{tt}groups@{utt} (id, name, description,
created_at, channel_count) and @{tt}count@{utt}.
@{bullet}list_channels: @{tt}channels@{utt} with every field of a channel
-- topic, context, status, verdict, metadata, times -- and @{tt}count@{utt}.
@{bullet}rename_channel: @{tt}status: renamed@{utt}, @{tt}old_name@{utt},
@{tt}new_name@{utt}.
@{bullet}conclude: @{tt}status: concluded@{utt} and the summary kept. A
channel may be concluded again; the new verdict replaces the old one.
@{bullet}reopen_channel: @{tt}status: reopened@{utt} and the name. The
verdict stays with the channel until it is concluded again.
@{bullet}A refusal is @{tt}{"error": "..."}@{utt}: a missing parameter,
"Channel 7 not found", "Channel 7 is already active", "Group 9 not found
(see list_groups)".

Groups cannot be renamed or deleted. A channel is deleted only in the
panel, with all its posts.
@endnode

@node posts "Posts and threads"
@{h1}Posts and threads

@{h2}The tools

@{table}
Tool | Needs | Does
post_message | content; channel_id and agent_name for a new post | a post, or a further chunk of one
get_thread | channel_id | the whole debate, as text or as data
pin_message | message_id | pins or unpins a post
@{body}

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Used by | Meaning
channel_id | post_message, get_thread | the channel
agent_name | post_message | who speaks, as the others shall call them
agent_role | post_message | the speaker's role: critic, reviewer, moderator
round | post_message | the round, 1, 2, 3 ...; without it 1
content | post_message | the text, or the next chunk with append
append | post_message | true: add content to the end of message_id instead of posting
message_id | post_message, pin_message | the post to append to, or to pin
metadata | post_message | any JSON object, kept with the post
format | get_thread | text (default) or json
max_messages | get_thread | only the first so many posts, oldest first; 0 or none: all
pinned | pin_message | false unpins; without it the post is pinned
@{body}

@{h2}Posting

A post goes only into an active channel. A new post shorter than the
server's @{tt}min_message_length@{utt} (10 characters in the shipped
setup, spaces at the ends not counted) is refused: a post of two words is
nearly always a cut-off answer, and once written nobody can tell it from
a real one.

Long output goes in chunks: the first as a normal post, each further one
with @{tt}append: true@{utt} and the @{tt}message_id@{utt} the first
answer returned. The plugin joins them into one post, exactly as sent --
continue where the last chunk ended. Chunks are not held to the minimum
length. A participant that was given the post before a chunk arrived gets
the chunk as a continuation (see @{" The debate context hook " link hook}).

A parameter sent as @{tt}null@{utt} counts as left out. @{tt}append@{utt}
and @{tt}pinned@{utt} also take "true" and "false" as text.

@{h2}Answers

@{bullet}post_message: @{tt}status: posted@{utt}, @{tt}message_id@{utt},
@{tt}channel_id@{utt}; with append @{tt}status: appended@{utt} and the
post's @{tt}length@{utt} so far.
@{bullet}get_thread as text: @{tt}thread@{utt} and @{tt}message_count@{utt}
-- the number of posts in the channel, also when
@{tt}max_messages@{utt} returned fewer. As json: @{tt}channel@{utt}, the
@{tt}messages@{utt} with every field, and their count.
@{bullet}pin_message: @{tt}status: pinned@{utt} or
@{tt}unpinned@{utt}.
@{bullet}Refusals: "Channel 7 is concluded, cannot post", "Message too
short (5 chars, min 10). Likely a truncated API response. Please provide
full message.", "append=true requires 'message_id' and 'content'",
"Message 12 not found".

The text of @{tt}get_thread@{utt}:

@{code text}
FORUM DEBATE: api-design-options
TOPIC: Which of the three API designs is best?

CONTEXT:
Three drafts, see below.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

[REVIEWER "Ada" | Round 1]
Draft B keeps the resources flat ...

────────────────────────────────────────
VERDICT: Draft B, with the paging of draft A.
@{body}

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"name": "api-design-options", "topic": "Which of the three API designs is best?"}
{"channel_id": 7, "agent_name": "Ada", "agent_role": "reviewer", "round": 1, "content": "Draft B keeps the resources flat ..."}
{"message_id": 31, "pinned": true}
{"channel_id": 7, "verdict": {"winner": "B", "score": 8}, "summary": "Draft B, with the paging of draft A."}
@{body}

These are the arguments of create_channel, post_message, pin_message and
conclude in turn.
@endnode

@node hook "The debate context hook"
@{h1}The debate context hook

@{tt}inject_debate_context@{utt} runs before each LLM call and keeps a
participant up to date with the debate, so it never has to read the
thread. It is off until an agent switches it on, and it does something
only in a session with a debate channel: the session variable
@{tt}debate_channel_id@{utt}. A moderator sets it (with a task_switch
tool's @{tt}set_context@{utt}); the sub-agents it spawns afterwards
inherit it.

@{h2}What the model gets

Two things, each only when there is something new:

@{bullet}The channel block, as a developer message at the end of the
history: name, topic, context and every pinned post. It is added again
whenever it changes -- a post pinned or unpinned -- or is no longer in
the history, for example after the conversation was compressed.

@{code markdown}
## Debate Forum – Channel #7 (api-design-options)
**Topic:** Which of the three API designs is best?
**Context:** Three drafts, see below.

📌 **Pinned messages (always visible):**

<post author="Kai" role="moderator" pinned="true">
The task: choose one design and say why.
</post>
@{body}

@{bullet}The new posts, as a user message placed in front of the message
being answered -- or, once the model has answered or called a tool in
this turn, at the end, behind what was sent already: every post not pinned that the session has not been
given yet, grouped by round. A post that has grown since it was given --
a further chunk appended -- comes again with only its new part, marked
@{tt}continuation_of@{utt} that post.

@{code markdown}
[Debate-Forum Channel #7 – New posts]

### Round 2

<post author="Ada" role="reviewer" round="2" msg_id="34">
Draft B keeps the resources flat ...
</post>
@{body}

What the session has been given, the plugin notes in one session
variable, @{tt}debate_counters@{utt}: per channel each post and how much
of it. It lives outside the message list, so it holds after compression.
A note the session inherited from the agent that spawned it counts as
none: a new sub-agent is given the whole thread first. A session moved to
another channel and back is given only what it has not seen in either.

Earlier batches stay in the conversation and are compressed with it like
any other message; the hook keeps no window of its own. Nothing is ever
rewritten: the cached prompt in front of a new block stays as it was.

@{h2}Switching it on

In the participant's YAML:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    enabled: true
    overrides:
      debate_forum.inject_debate_context:
        enabled: true
@{body}

The hook has no settings.
@endnode

@node direct "Direct messages between sessions"
@{h1}Direct messages between sessions

Two tools let an agent write to another of your sessions that is running,
the way one terminal hands a note to another. Which session runs where,
and starting one that is idle, belong to the core's session presence; it
has to be on (@{tt}session_presence.enabled@{utt} in
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}, on in the shipped setup). Without it both
tools answer "Session presence is off (session_presence.enabled in
config.yaml)".

@{h2}The tools

@{table}
Tool | Parameters | Does
list_sessions | -- | your other sessions that are running or being started, with session_id, status and agent
send_message | to, message | sends the message to the session with that id
@{body}

@{tt}to@{utt} is a session id: from list_sessions, the
@{tt}from_session@{utt} of a message received, or any other saved session
of the same user -- an idle one is started to read it. An unknown id, a
session of another user or the agent's own is refused: "No session 'x' to
message (see list_sessions)". Sessions of sub-agents are not listed and
never started: a message to one waits for its next run, which the run
that spawned it starts.

@{h2}What send_message answers

@{tt}{"status": ..., "message_id": ...}@{utt}, with a @{tt}note@{utt} when
there is a reason to give:

@{table}
status | Means
delivered_next_step | the session is running; it reads the message on its next step
woke_session | the session was idle and has been started to read it
being_woken | a start is already on its way and will read it
queued | it waits for the session's next run; the note says why (stopped by its user, wake chain limit, not allowed to run)
unknown | the session vanished in the meantime
@{body}

@{h2}How it arrives

The message is stored as a post in the pair's channel (group @{i}Direct
messages@{ui}, visible in the panel). The hook
@{tt}deliver_direct_messages@{utt} appends it to the recipient's next LLM
call as a user message:

@{code markdown}
[Direct messages -- reply with debate_forum_send_message to the from_session]
<message from_session="3f2a…" from_agent="coder">
The tests pass on my branch; can you review?
</message>
@{body}

An agent without the send_message tool gets the same message under a
plain @{tt}[Direct messages]@{utt} line, without the hint.

It counts as delivered once the conversation that carried it has been
saved (the hook @{tt}mark_direct_messages_delivered@{utt}, at the end of
the session's run). A run that dies before, or is not saved, leaves it
undelivered and the next run gets it again -- it may then be read twice,
but it is never lost. Within one run it is given only once.

Both hooks are on by default. An agent that shall not receive direct
messages turns delivery off in its YAML:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    overrides:
      debate_forum.deliver_direct_messages:
        enabled: false
@{body}
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The tool descriptions and every parameter's description come from the
plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. A refused call answers
@{tt}{"error": "..."}@{utt} with the reason ("Channel 7 is concluded,
cannot post", "name and topic are required", "channel_id and verdict are
required"). In the chat, the call's status line ends with the result:
"Channel #7 'api-design-options' created", "Posted to #7 (round 2,
id=34, 412 chars) -- Ada [reviewer]", "Appended 3000 chars to message 34
(now 6000)", "Listed 4 channel(s) [status=active]", "Channel #7
concluded", "Message #35 to 3f2a…: woke_session".

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Tool answers are ordinary tool results: appended, nothing earlier changes.
@{tt}get_thread@{utt} returns the whole debate with every post in full
-- on a long debate that is many tokens; a participant with the hook on
does not need it. @{tt}list_channels@{utt} returns each channel's context
and verdict: a small @{tt}limit@{utt} keeps it short.

The hooks append: the channel block at the end, new posts in front of the
message being answered (at the end once the turn has an answer or a tool
call behind it, which a move would take out of the cache), direct
messages at the end. None of them rewrites
the system prompt or an earlier message, so the cached prompt before them
stays valid.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    debate_forum:
      type: debate_forum
      enabled: true
      config:
        db_path: "data/debate_forum/forum.db"
        min_message_length: 10  # 0 = disabled
@{body}

An agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+debate_forum/*"
@{body}

The debate agents bring their own sub-agent manager (and
debate_panel_moderator its task_switch) in their YAMLs. Both moderators
and the participants of debate_panel_moderator also use context_engineer,
those participants tavily_search as well; panel_participant has no tools.
Direct messages need
session presence (see @{" Direct messages between sessions " link direct}).

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
db_path | debate_forum/forum.db in the data directory | the forum's database
min_message_length | 50 | the shortest new post the tool takes; 0 no limit
@{body}

Everything -- groups, channels, posts, direct messages -- is one SQLite
file. Deleting a channel removes its posts; there is no other clean-up.
@endnode
